WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:03.120
<v Instructor>So let's have a look at Amazon EventBridge.</v>

2
00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:04.350
So you have several options.

3
00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:06.120
You can create a rule with an event pattern.

4
00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:07.590
We're gonna do this right now.

5
00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:08.730
Then you can have a schedule rule,

6
00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:10.110
which is the old way of doing schedule.

7
00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:12.000
So there is now something called EventBridge schedule.

8
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.710
We're also going to have a look at it.

9
00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:16.680
Pipe is to send data from an event source to target

10
00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.690
with optional filtering and enrichment.

11
00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:19.770
And schema registry is to look

12
00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:22.410
at the schemas of all these events.

13
00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:26.070
So just to get started, we're going to do the first option,

14
00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:27.840
EventBridge rule with event pattern.

15
00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:29.160
So we need to create a rule,

16
00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.580
and what we want to do is to react to any event

17
00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:36.480
where an instance is going to be terminated or shut down.

18
00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:39.180
So you click here on Service Events,

19
00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:42.030
and here you have a list at all the events

20
00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:44.970
that can happen within your accounts by service.

21
00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:46.320
So it's a lot.

22
00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.780
For example, if you take any of these,

23
00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:52.590
for example, if you take Lambda,

24
00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:54.360
you can have a look at all these things.

25
00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:55.574
Console actions or EC2,

26
00:00:55.574 --> 00:00:57.180
you can have a look at all these things.

27
00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:58.530
So it's a lot more type

28
00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:00.750
of things you can choose from for events.

29
00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:02.040
So the one we're going to look

30
00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:03.360
at is one of the most popular.

31
00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:07.350
It's called EC2 instance State-change Notification.

32
00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:08.880
So that means that anytime

33
00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:11.190
an EC2 instance state will change,

34
00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:12.330
we're going to get a notification.

35
00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:14.790
So if we look at it at this event,

36
00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:17.040
we can have a look at the schema of the event

37
00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:18.150
and then a sample event.

38
00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:19.830
Like what it can look like.

39
00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:24.270
So the schema defines how the sample event will look like.

40
00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:27.330
And now we want to be able to filter this event

41
00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:29.130
and on the state value.

42
00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:32.730
So the state is a string and we have to exit the value.

43
00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:36.990
So we can say Equal, for example,

44
00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:41.040
and we are going to have several values.

45
00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:43.090
So the first one is shutting down

46
00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:48.783
and the second one is going to be terminated.

47
00:01:49.851 --> 00:01:52.650
How do I know this? Well, it's from the schema itself.

48
00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:56.130
If you have a look at the event pattern on the bottom line,

49
00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:57.780
on the bottom page, sorry,

50
00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:01.350
you can have a look at Sample events 1, 2, 3.

51
00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:02.550
And if you look at number 5,

52
00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:05.070
it says "state": "shutting-down".

53
00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:07.170
And number 6, it says "state": "terminated".

54
00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:09.870
So these are the events we want to be catching.

55
00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:13.860
So now our event pattern filter is defined right here

56
00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:15.510
on the right-hand side.

57
00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:17.460
So now we have all the events.

58
00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:20.550
And now for the target, again, we have several options.

59
00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:22.901
So we can have a look at all the options right here,

60
00:02:22.901 --> 00:02:25.980
such as API gateway, batch jobs.

61
00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:29.760
We can also reboot an EC2 instance, terminate one.

62
00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:33.330
We have SNS topic, SQS queues, I mean so many.

63
00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:35.220
But what we want to do right now is to receive an alert,

64
00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:37.800
so we choose SNS Topic

65
00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:40.093
and then we want it to be our demo topic.

66
00:02:40.093 --> 00:02:41.910
So create it if you don't have a topic.

67
00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:44.610
You can just create an SNS topic very easily.

68
00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:46.440
Then we'll need to have an exception role to be able

69
00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:48.120
to send a message into this topic.

70
00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:50.910
So a role is going to be automatically created for us.

71
00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:52.950
and then we get some more information

72
00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:55.350
around the retry policy and the dead-letter queue.

73
00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:57.660
So we're good to go. Let's click on Create.

74
00:02:57.660 --> 00:02:59.280
I'll call this one

75
00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:00.713
NotifyEC2InstanceShutdownOrTerminated,

76
00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:07.980
and we'll click on Create.

77
00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:09.693
And so now that this role has been created,

78
00:03:09.693 --> 00:03:11.280
anytime we shut down

79
00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:13.020
or terminate an EC2 instance,

80
00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:15.900
we will receive a notification.

81
00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:17.370
The other option is to use Schedule.

82
00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:19.650
So on the left-hand side you have schedules,

83
00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:21.600
and then you can create a schedule.

84
00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:24.213
And this one is going called InvokeLambdaEveryHour.

85
00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:28.260
And several options.

86
00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:29.460
Either it's a one-time schedule,

87
00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:33.030
so we do something once and then done, or recurring.

88
00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:36.300
And we have either a Cron-based schedule or a rate schedule.

89
00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:37.900
We'll use 1 hour

90
00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:41.703
and no flexible time window.

91
00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:44.550
And Next.

92
00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:46.770
And here we have the option to invoke several things.

93
00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:49.050
So what do we wanna do every hour?

94
00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:51.210
Well, we have all these options such as, for example,

95
00:03:51.210 --> 00:03:53.310
running a task on Amazon ECS

96
00:03:53.310 --> 00:03:55.620
or putting a records in Kinesis Data Firehose

97
00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:57.720
or invoking a Lambda function.

98
00:03:57.720 --> 00:03:59.160
So you can say, for example,

99
00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:00.750
in the Invoke Lambda function,

100
00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:02.070
and if you have a Lambda function,

101
00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:03.510
you can select it from here.

102
00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:04.410
So you get the idea.

103
00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:05.400
Once you've done this,

104
00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:08.010
you have a schedule in Amazon EventBridge.

105
00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:11.250
Other things that you need to be looking at is event buses.

106
00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:13.560
So right now we have the default event bus.

107
00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:15.535
And the default events bus is anything

108
00:04:15.535 --> 00:04:19.050
that will be AWS-generated type of events.

109
00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:21.270
But you can create your own event bus,

110
00:04:21.270 --> 00:04:23.670
and this is going to be a custom event bus.

111
00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:26.160
And here you have the ability yourself

112
00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:28.587
to send your own events into this event bus

113
00:04:28.587 --> 00:04:31.440
and so therefore you can develop custom applications

114
00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:33.750
that are based on Amazon EventBridge,

115
00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:35.610
which can be very helpful.

116
00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:39.090
You have the option to archive your events.

117
00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:40.320
When they happen on the event bus,

118
00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:42.570
then you have an archive you can look into.

119
00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:44.640
And you can also replay events if you wanted

120
00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:47.673
to replay a past event from an archive.

121
00:04:48.630 --> 00:04:50.010
Okay, other things need you look at

122
00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:51.870
is partner event sources.

123
00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:53.190
So it's possible for you

124
00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:56.640
to actually get data from third-party partners directly

125
00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:58.920
into your AWS accounts.

126
00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:01.290
So if you use, for example, Auth0,

127
00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:03.900
then the events of Auth0 will be put

128
00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:05.610
and inserted into Amazon EventBridge.

129
00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:08.100
And then you can, for example, use a Lambda function

130
00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:11.370
or do something after someone has done the login in of zero.

131
00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:13.050
But you can see you have so many different kinds

132
00:05:13.050 --> 00:05:15.000
of partners right here.

133
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:16.050
And API destinations.

134
00:05:16.050 --> 00:05:17.820
That means that, for example, if you wanted

135
00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:19.770
to have an event within EventBridge,

136
00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:24.060
but then to connect to an external HTP destination,

137
00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:26.130
you can also define one here.

138
00:05:26.130 --> 00:05:28.380
And this allows you to connect EventBridge

139
00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:30.330
to an outside source, for example,

140
00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:32.640
to have integration with your own infrastructure.

141
00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:34.320
So it's quite a lot of different things.

142
00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:37.530
And finally, for schemas, this is all the schemas

143
00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:40.170
of all the AWS events that you have.

144
00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:41.730
Or you can have your own custom registry

145
00:05:41.730 --> 00:05:43.050
for your own events.

146
00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:45.210
But this allows you to understand the shape

147
00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:48.600
and form the events will have in Amazon EventBridge.

148
00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:50.160
And that's it. So I hope you liked it.

